{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_418","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -C 3 'exit.interview|Source Information|Testing Feedback|Engine Feedback|Prompt Feedback|README|clean|sim\\\\b|test' TASK.md | tail -180\nprintf '\\\\nProgress and files:\\\\n'\ncat PROGRESS.md\nprintf '\\\\n'; ls -la 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701' | head -35\nprintf '\\\\nPlan headings:\\\\n'; rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} '\"' PLAN.md | head -100\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_418","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -C 3 'exit.interview|Source Information|Testing Feedback|Engine Feedback|Prompt Feedback|README|clean|sim\\\\b|test' TASK.md | tail -180\nprintf '\\\\nProgress and files:\\\\n'\ncat PROGRESS.md\nprintf '\\\\n'; ls -la 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701' | head -35\nprintf '\\\\nPlan headings:\\\\n'; rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} '\"' PLAN.md | head -100\"","aggregated_output":"71-\n72-Create a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73-\n74:Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75-\n76-Attract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77-\n--\n89-## Done\n90-No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n91-\n92:## Source Information\n93:CRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94-- Date: 2026-11-04 22:27:01\n95-- Source: the work it's based on\n96-- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n--\n100-- Animation: system used\n101-- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102-- Online: network features used, or none\n103:- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104-- Research: research done, or none\n105-- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n106-- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-terra, reasoning effort max\n107-- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108-\n109:Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110-{\n111:  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112:  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113:  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114:  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115-  \"aiHub\": {\n116-    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n117-    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n--\n127-}\n128-The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129-\n130:Testing Feedback\n131-\n132:- How many issues did testing catch?\n133-- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134:- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135:- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136-\n137:Engine Feedback\n138-\n139-- What did you like most about the engine?\n140-- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n--\n143-- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n144-- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145-\n146:Prompt Feedback\n147-\n148-- What did you like most about this prompt?\n149-- What was confusing?\n\nProgress and files:\n# Progress\n\n- Title: **Wii Fit Together**, selected uniformly using Node.js `crypto.randomInt` from 50 brainstormed candidates.\n- Deliverable: `Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701.html` in the workspace root; source and development files in the identically named directory.\n- Engine and Hub client acquired; native engine build succeeds. Registered a Tippy Cay scene and deterministic balance spring in Shardfall.\n- The standalone build is bundled. All 24 phone, tablet, desktop and cross-browser viewport launches passed after fixing a startup injection bug.\n- The browser suite passed ten checks, including three engines, the five-day stay, save persistence, the local medley, and 2–4-player AI Hub runs. Two Rust balance-spring tests passed.\n- First-clear and repeat minute rewards now follow the plan. Rebuild the HTML, re-run the changed browser suite, and complete the exit interview.\n- Limitation: the playable presentation and most activity logic are Canvas 2D / JavaScript over a Shardfall hook, not the planned full native 3D engine implementation.\n\ntotal 11104\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 23:02 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 23:02 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 17 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 22:31 engine\n-rw-r--r--  1 micha micha 11341115 Nov  4 23:02 game.raw.html\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 22:32 hub\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 23:02 scripts\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 23:13 tests\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 micha micha     4096 Nov  4 22:50 web\n\nPlan headings:\n1:# Game plan: a balance-and-wellness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n5:## 0. How to read this plan\n15:## 1. The game in one page\n48:## 2. Structure of play\n74:## 3. Controls\n94:## 4. The Balance Core\n119:## 5. Events\n125:### Stillness Studio (Posey). Set: `studio`, a round white deck on a calm dawn lagoon. Camera: mirror view (yaw 0, tilt 12, distance 8.5, fov 36; the figure faces +Z, toward the camera, and stick right is screen right).\n131:### Iron Gazebo (Big Kettle). Set: `gazebo`, a white iron gazebo at golden hour. Camera: 3/4 front.\n137:### Boardwalk (Bounce). Set: `boardwalk`, a beach boardwalk at bright midday.\n145:### Lagoon (Commodore Gull). Sets: `tide`, `channel`, `pier`, `tower`.\n155:### The Big Stand (finale, about 2.5 minutes). Set: `bay`, a tiny post in the bay at sunset, with the whole cast watching from rafts.\n166:### Morning Wobble Check. Set: `studio`. About 70 s, four micro-tests, Pip presiding.\n181:## 6. Meta systems\n192:## 7. World, cast and writing\n227:## 8. Art direction\n258:# The sea: water zones near the action, one huge disc beyond them. Fog hides its edge.\n283:## 9. Animation\n308:## 10. Audio\n333:## 11. UI and UX\n371:## 12. Attract mode\n377:## 13. Multiplayer\n443:## 14. Architecture\n520:## 15. Verified facts and traps\n557:## 16. Testing\n582:## 17. Build order\n600:## 18. Finishing\n608:### What each brief item maps to\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
