{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_422","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs'\nprintf '\\\\nBrowser startup wrapper:\\\\n'; rg -n 'requestAdapter|requestDevice|wasm_bindgen|navigator.gpu|WebGPU|webgpu' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/scripts' | head -90\nprintf '\\\\nTask requirements:\\\\n'; sed -n '1,165p' TASK.md\nprintf '\\\\nFinish instructions:\\\\n'; sed -n '443,520p' PLAN.md; sed -n '557,608p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_422","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs'\nprintf '\\\\nBrowser startup wrapper:\\\\n'; rg -n 'requestAdapter|requestDevice|wasm_bindgen|navigator.gpu|WebGPU|webgpu' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/scripts' | head -90\nprintf '\\\\nTask requirements:\\\\n'; sed -n '1,165p' TASK.md\nprintf '\\\\nFinish instructions:\\\\n'; sed -n '443,520p' PLAN.md; sed -n '557,608p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"//! Window-bound graphics: surface, device, scene renderer and egui renderer.\n\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Context, Result, anyhow};\nuse pav_render::gpu::{self, BackendChoice};\nuse pav_render::wgpu;\nuse pav_render::{Renderer, Scene};\nuse winit::window::Window;\n\nuse crate::boot::{stage, stage_async};\n\npub struct Gfx {\n    pub window: Arc<Window>,\n    pub surface: wgpu::Surface<'static>,\n    pub adapter: wgpu::Adapter,\n    pub device: wgpu::Device,\n    pub queue: wgpu::Queue,\n    pub config: wgpu::SurfaceConfiguration,\n    pub renderer: Renderer,\n    pub egui: egui_wgpu::Renderer,\n    pub vsync: bool,\n    pub adapter_name: String,\n    pub gpu_errors: Arc<std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32>,\n}\n\nimpl Gfx {\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n    pub fn new(window: Arc<Window>, backend: BackendChoice, vsync: bool) -> Result<Self> {\n        pollster_block(Self::create(window, backend, vsync))\n    }\n\n    /// Creates everything; only the adapter and device requests wait (in the browser).\n    pub async fn create(window: Arc<Window>, backend: BackendChoice, vsync: bool) -> Result<Self> {\n        let web = cfg!(target_arch = \"wasm32\");\n        let instance = stage(\"graphics instance\", || {\n            Ok((gpu::create_instance(backend), format!(\"backend: {}\", if web { \"webgpu\" } else { backend.name() })))\n        })?;\n        let surface = stage(\"window surface\", || {\n            let s = instance.create_surface(window.clone()).context(\"could not create a drawing surface for the window\")?;\n            Ok((s, String::new()))\n        })?;\n        let adapter = stage_async(\"graphics adapter\", async {\n            if !web {\n                let all = gpu::list_adapters(&instance, backend);\n                for a in &all {\n                    log::info!(\"  found adapter: {}\", gpu::describe(a));\n                }\n                if all.is_empty() {\n                    return Err(anyhow!(\n                        \"no {} adapter found. Update your graphics driver{}\",\n                        backend.name(),\n                        if backend == BackendChoice::Vulkan { \", or try backend = \\\"dx12\\\" in shardfall.toml\" } else { \"\" }\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n            let a = gpu::request_adapter_async(&instance, Some(&surface)).await.map_err(|e| {\n                if web { anyhow!(\"{e:#}. This page needs a browser with WebGPU (a recent Chrome or Edge)\") } else { e }\n            })?;\n            let d = gpu::describe(&a.get_info());\n            Ok((a, d))\n        })\n        .await?;\n        let (device, queue) = stage_async(\"graphics device\", async {\n            let (d, q) = gpu::request_device_async(&adapter).await?;\n            Ok(((d, q), String::new()))\n        })\n        .await?;\n        let gpu_errors = Arc::new(std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32::new(0));\n        {\n            let errs = gpu_errors.clone();\n            device.on_uncaptured_error(Arc::new(move |e| {\n                errs.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed);\n                log::error!(\"GPU error: {e}\");\n            }));\n            device.set_device_lost_callback(|reason, msg| log::error!(\"GPU device lost ({reason:?}): {msg}\"));\n        }\n        let size = window.inner_size();\n        let config = stage(\"surface configure\", || {\n            let caps = surface.get_capabilities(&adapter);\n            let mut c = surface\n                .get_default_config(&adapter, size.width.max(1), size.height.max(1))\n                .context(\"surface not supported by adapter\")?;\n            // Non-sRGB target: the post pass encodes sRGB itself, and egui expects a linear-blend target.\n            c.format = caps.formats.iter().copied().find(|f| !f.is_srgb()).unwrap_or(c.format);\n            c.present_mode = present_mode(&caps, vsync);\n            surface.configure(&device, &c);\n            Ok((c.clone(), format!(\"{:?}, {:?}, {}x{}\", c.format, c.present_mode, c.width, c.height)))\n        })?;\n        let scope = device.push_error_scope(wgpu::ErrorFilter::Validation);\n        let renderer = stage(\"renderer & shaders\", || Ok((Renderer::new(&device, &queue), String::new())))?;\n        if let Some(e) = scope.pop().await {\n            return Err(anyhow!(\"shader/pipeline creation failed: {e}\"));\n        }\n        let egui = stage(\"ui renderer\", || {\n            Ok((egui_wgpu::Renderer::new(&device, config.format, egui_wgpu::RendererOptions::default()), String::new()))\n        })?;\n        let adapter_name = gpu::describe(&adapter.get_info());\n        Ok(Self { window, surface, adapter, device, queue, config, renderer, egui, vsync, adapter_name, gpu_errors })\n    }\n\n    pub fn resize(&mut self, w: u32, h: u32) {\n        if w == 0 || h == 0 {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.config.width = w;\n        self.config.height = h;\n        self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n    }\n\n    pub fn set_vsync(&mut self, on: bool) {\n        self.vsync = on;\n        let caps = self.surface.get_capabilities(&self.adapter);\n        self.config.present_mode = present_mode(&caps, on);\n        self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n    }\n\n    /// Acquires the next surface texture, reconfiguring when needed. None = skip this frame.\n    pub fn acquire(&mut self) -> Option<wgpu::SurfaceTexture> {\n        match self.surface.get_current_texture() {\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Success(t) => Some(t),\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Suboptimal(t) => {\n                // Use this frame, reconfigure for the next one.\n                self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n                Some(t)\n            }\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Outdated | wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Lost => {\n                self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n                None\n            }\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn size(&self) -> (u32, u32) {\n        (self.config.width, self.config.height)\n    }\n\n    /// Renders the scene plus the egui overlay and presents. Texture updates are always\n    /// applied (and the delta cleared), even when the frame is skipped.\n    pub fn draw(&mut self, scene: &Scene, ui: Option<(&[egui::ClippedPrimitive], &mut egui::TexturesDelta, f32)>) -> bool {\n        let mut ui = ui;\n        if let Some((_, textures, _)) = ui.as_mut() {\n            for (id, deltas) in &textures.set {\n                for delta in deltas {\n                    self.egui.update_texture(&self.device, &self.queue, *id, delta);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let frame = self.acquire();\n        let submitted = frame.is_some();\n        if let Some(frame) = frame {\n            let view = frame.texture.create_view(&wgpu::TextureViewDescriptor::default());\n            let mut encoder = self.device.create_command_encoder(&wgpu::CommandEncoderDescriptor { label: Some(\"frame\") });\n            let size = self.size();\n            self.renderer.render(&mut encoder, scene, &view, self.config.format, size);\n            let mut cmds = Vec::new();\n            if let Some((prims, _, ppp)) = ui.as_ref() {\n                let sd = egui_wgpu::ScreenDescriptor { size_in_pixels: [size.0, size.1], pixels_per_point: *ppp };\n                cmds = self.egui.update_buffers(&self.device, &self.queue, &mut encoder, prims, &sd);\n                let pass = encoder.begin_render_pass(&wgpu::RenderPassDescriptor {\n                    label: Some(\"egui\"),\n                    color_attachments: &[Some(wgpu::RenderPassColorAttachment {\n                        view: &view,\n                        depth_slice: None,\n                        resolve_target: None,\n                        ops: wgpu::Operations { load: wgpu::LoadOp::Load, store: wgpu::StoreOp::Store },\n                    })],\n                    depth_stencil_attachment: None,\n                    timestamp_writes: None,\n                    occlusion_query_set: None,\n                    multiview_mask: None,\n                });\n                self.egui.render(&mut pass.forget_lifetime(), prims, &sd);\n            }\n            self.queue.submit(cmds.into_iter().chain([encoder.finish()]));\n            self.window.pre_present_notify();\n            self.queue.present(frame);\n        }\n        if let Some((_, textures, _)) = ui {\n            for id in &textures.free {\n                self.egui.free_texture(id);\n            }\n            textures.clear();\n        }\n        submitted\n    }\n}\n\nfn present_mode(caps: &wgpu::SurfaceCapabilities, vsync: bool) -> wgpu::PresentMode {\n    let want: &[wgpu::PresentMode] = if vsync {\n        &[wgpu::PresentMode::Fifo]\n    } else {\n        &[wgpu::PresentMode::Mailbox, wgpu::PresentMode::Immediate, wgpu::PresentMode::Fifo]\n    };\n    want.iter().copied().find(|m| caps.present_modes.contains(m)).unwrap_or(caps.present_modes[0])\n}\n\n#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\nfn pollster_block<F: std::future::Future>(f: F) -> F::Output {\n    // Minimal executor (the futures here resolve immediately on native).\n    use std::task::{Context as Cx, Poll, RawWaker, RawWakerVTable, Waker};\n    fn noop(_: *const ()) {}\n    fn clone(p: *const ()) -> RawWaker {\n        RawWaker::new(p, &VTABLE)\n    }\n    static VTABLE: RawWakerVTable = RawWakerVTable::new(clone, noop, noop, noop);\n    let waker = unsafe { Waker::from_raw(RawWaker::new(std::ptr::null(), &VTABLE)) };\n    let mut cx = Cx::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut f = std::pin::pin!(f);\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = f.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n        std::thread::yield_now();\n    }\n}\n\nBrowser startup wrapper:\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/scripts/bundle.py:24:<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">'''\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/scripts/bundle.py:30:html = html.replace(\"if (!navigator.gpu) {\", \"if (!navigator.gpu || !(await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter().catch(() => null))) {\", 1)\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/build-web.sh:2:# Builds the browser version (WebAssembly + WebGPU). Output: target/web/{index.html,manifest.webmanifest,shardfall.js,shardfall_bg.wasm}\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:2:# Compiles every WGSL module the renderer uses with the browser's WebGPU (Tint, in headless\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:35:  // WebGPU needs a secure context: serve a blank page from localhost.\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:42:    args: ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'],\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:49:      if (!navigator.gpu) return { fatal: 'navigator.gpu is missing (WebGPU off?)' };\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:50:      const adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter();\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:51:      if (!adapter) return { fatal: 'no WebGPU adapter' };\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/scripts/check-wgsl.sh:52:      const device = await adapter.requestDevice();\n\nTask requirements:\nSOURCE: Wii Fit\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall/blob/main/START_HERE.md, making a new game on its engine. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:27:01\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-terra, reasoning effort max\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n\nFinish instructions:\n## 14. Architecture\n\n**Decision: all game state and flow live in the sim, in one `Game`.** Menus, dialogue, pause, settings values and dev toggles are state in `FitGame`, driven by `InputFrame`, so `cargo test` can play the whole game with no browser or GPU in seconds. `pav_ui` is a pure view of a `UiFrame`: it draws the frame, and returns what the pointer hovered and clicked, which the app sends back as `InputFrame.ui` (clear PRIMARY that frame if the UI took the click) and publishes as item rects in `window.__gameUi` for browser tests. Pause freezes the world by an early return in `Sim::step_inner`, not by `TimeControl`.\n\n```\nengine/crates/pav_core/src/games/fit/\n  mod.rs           FitGame: Flow state machine, Game impl, build(), scene routing\n  core.rs          BalanceCore\n  intent.rs        InputFrame to per-event intent\n  events/*.rs      one file per event, dispatched through an enum (Clone + serde)\n  check.rs finale.rs medley.rs meta.rs dialog.rs ghost.rs attract.rs ui_model.rs\n  rules.toml       every balance number (hot-reloads via level_text)\n  script.toml      all text\n  charts.toml      Step Party notes\n  sets/*.toml      hub studio gazebo boardwalk tide channel pier tower bay\nengine/anim/fit.json                 hand-keyed clips\nengine/assets/props/lib/core/*.json  props\nengine/crates/pav_ui/src/fit_ui.rs, glyphs.rs, fonts/\nengine/crates/pav_audio/src/music.rs, voice.rs\nengine/crates/pav_app/src/weblink.rs  window field reads and writes\nengine/web/index.html                 hub client, tags, JS, about-data\ntests/  tools/  hub/  notes/  README.md  (how to add an event, set or song)\n```\nRegister it as `entry::<fit::FitGame>(..., fit::build)`, `KEY = \"fit\"`. Scene names: `fit`, `fit/medley`, `fit/event/<name>`. Read data with `games::level_text(\"fit/rules\", include_str!(\"rules.toml\"))`: running from `engine/`, it reads the disk file, so text, numbers and set layouts change with no rebuild. Do not put sets in `engine/rooms/`: that folder feeds the pavilion demo.\n\n**Every set is a room on one `Sim`** (verified: two rooms, each with its own `[camera]` and `[view]`, switching live in the browser):\n\n```rust\nlet def = RoomDef::parse(&level_text(key, text)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"{key}: {e}\"))?;\nlet place = Placement::new(def.layout.origin, 0);       // give each set an origin 200 m apart\nlet (cols, rows) = def.layout.extent();\nlet (min, max) = place.aabb(Vec3::new(0.0, -1.0, 0.0), Vec3::new(cols as f32, def.height.max(2.0), rows as f32));\nlet start = def.local_start();\nlet inward = -def.entrance.facing.dir();\nrooms.push(RoomSlot { id: i as u16, key: key.into(), def: Arc::new(def), place, min, max,\n                      inside: place.point(start), outside: place.point(start) - inward * 4.0, inward, built: false });\n// then:\nsim.state.world = World { enabled: false, rooms, current_room: Some(0), ..Default::default() };\nfor i in 0..n { sim.build_room(i); }\n// markers() reads current_room: set it, read the markers, set it back.\n// To change set: sim.state.world.current_room = Some(i); sim.set_position(player, marker);\n```\nKeep player movement numbers in code, since `[params]` is not re-applied on a manual switch. The app applies the room's `[camera]` and `[view]` when the room id changes; set `self.arrival` there too, for the iris transition.\n\n**Per-state cameras:** `sim.state.courses.sticky_cue = Some(Arc::new(CameraCue { set, sweep, flip_ortho: 0.0 })); sim.state.courses.cue_serial += 1;` blends over 0.7 s in the window and in the tools. Use `sweep` for slow result-screen orbits. Lighting per state: `courses.view` and `view_serial`.\n\n| File | Change | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pav_core/src/input.rs` | Add to `InputFrame`: `lean: Vec2`, `any: bool`, `ui: Option<UiEvent>` (Copy: `Hover(u16)`, `Click(u16)`, `Slider(u16, f32)`), `song_pos: Option<f32>`, all `#[serde(default)]`. New `buttons`: CONFIRM, BACK, MENU_UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT, TAB_L, TAB_R, PAUSE, SCHEDULE, WAVE, STEP_L, STEP_R, HUB_GO | Everything the game needs is input, so replays and tests cover it |\n| `pav_core/src/games/mod.rs`, `sim.rs` | `Game::frozen()` with a default of false (add it to the trait and its `DynGame` and `GameBox` wrappers); when true, `step_inner` skips everything after `play_pre` except tick++ and events | Pause freezes the world while menus still run |\n| `pav_core/src/frame.rs`, `sim.rs` | `HudFrame.status: Option<String>` (the game's status JSON), `HudFrame.fit: Option<Arc<UiFrame>>` and `HudFrame.camera_yaw: Option<f32>`, which `pav_app` and `pav_tools/src/session.rs` apply to the rig each frame | Link to JS and to the UI; Morning Lap's chase camera, since cues blend over 0.7 s and cannot follow per tick |\n| `pav_view/src/build.rs`, `fx.rs` | Handle `BigSplash` (bigger water dents and bursts) and a silent `Wake { pos }` event (a small dent, no sound; `Splash` is loud if repeated) | The dive's payoff and the paddle's wake |\n| `pav_core/src/puppet.rs` | `tilt_side`, `tilt_fwd` in `PuppetState` (add to `lerp`), applied in `body_motion` as a rotation about the feet | The whole figure leans with the dot |\n| `pav_core/src/parts.rs` | `AttachKind::Handle` | Kettle |\n| `pav_ui/src/lib.rs`, `headless.rs`, `fit_ui.rs` | Draw `fit_ui` from both window and headless; `has_ui` true when `hud.fit` is set; install fonts before the first pass | Captures show the real UI |\n| `pav_tools/src/tools.rs` (`t_input`) | Accept `lean=[x,y]` | Poke events from the REPL |\n| `pav_app/src/input.rs` | `Bindings::fit()`, a fit layout in `poll_gamepad` (Cross, Circle, L1/L2, R1/R2, D-pad taps, Options unless Create is held), key ramps for lean, menu repeat | Controller-first layout |\n| `pav_app/src/app.rs` | When `hud.fit` is set: hide `boot_panel`, `stats_panel`, `guide_panel`, the room card, `hint_bar` and the F1 panel (verified necessary); route Esc and Options to PAUSE instead of `set_menu`; gate `handle_key`; call `weblink` each frame; apply Settings to audio buses; save to `localStorage` when the save serial changes; keep fit bindings in the room-change branch, which otherwise rebinds keys | Removes the engine demo's chrome and keys |\n| `pav_audio` | Section 10 | Music, voices, buses |\n| `web/index.html` | Client, tags, JS, `about-data`, catalogue `<title>Name — Tagline</title>`, description, theme colour, an inline SVG icon; remove the phone hints | Hub and catalogue |\n\n**In `pre`, always return the player's input with action bits removed:** `out.insert(pid, InputFrame { move_dir, ..Default::default() })`. Otherwise left click and F throw bombs, Space jumps and C crouches. The game replaces the player's own buttons (read in `sim.rs`).\n\n**Rust and JS link, no new dependencies (verified compiling and running):**\n```rust\nuse web_sys::js_sys::{Array, Reflect};\nuse web_sys::wasm_bindgen::{JsCast, JsValue};\nlet win = web_sys::window().unwrap();\nReflect::set(&win, &JsValue::from_str(\"__gameStatus\"), &JsValue::from_str(&status)).ok();\nlet v = Reflect::get(&win, &JsValue::from_str(\"__hub\")).unwrap_or(JsValue::UNDEFINED);\n// Array::from(&v) makes a copy, so clearing it leaves the original full (verified).\n// To read and clear a JS array in place: let arr: Array = v.unchecked_into(); ... arr.set_length(0);\n```\n`pack_single_html.py` refuses wasm-bindgen snippets (`inline_js`, `module`), so use `Reflect`. Do not use `host.exec` or `host.query` per frame: in the browser they re-publish a frame and rotate prev/curr, which stutters the interpolation. Read `curr.hud.status` instead. `Settings::load` should read `window.__gameBoot.scene` and `.seed` when present.\n\n**About text stays in plain text in the page.** Put it in `<script id=\"about-data\" type=\"application/json\">` in the `<head>`, not in the wasm. The agent that imports the game edits the Usage line later, and it cannot edit text inside a gzipped wasm. Rust reads it from `__gameBoot.about`.\n\n## 15. Verified facts and traps\n## 16. Testing\n\nOne command: `tests/run.sh [core|browser|hub|all]`. It prints only failures and a final line like `212 passed, 0 failed`. Rerun a part only after changing code it covers. Keep a running count in `notes/feedback.md` of issues found and how bad, so your testing feedback is honest.\n\n**`core` (`cargo test -q --locked -p pav_core --test fit`, then `cargo test -q --locked -p pav_audio -p pav_ui`; seconds to run once built):**\n- The bot plays New Stay to credits on 3 seeds: outcome Won, 5 stamps, every event played, under 60 sim-minutes.\n- Each event: the bot earns at least 2 stars on 5 seeds; idle input earns under 1 star; a careless random input earns less than the bot.\n- Balance Core: settle time, tip after `tip_time`, keyboard ramp, stick curve.\n- Morning Check: better input gives a lower age; bounds hold.\n- Attract: starts at 600 idle ticks, cycles all events, any input exits, saves nothing.\n- Pause freezes the world. Settings: music defaults to 0.10. Dev sliders change values and reset. Dev jump lands where it says.\n- Save round trip and version. Medley on a seed gives the same score for the same inputs. Play Together completes. Ghosts match by area.\n- Snapshot, rewind and replay exactness (copy `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs`).\n- UI: `Headless::frame` readings show the right prompts per device and glyph setting; no text under the size rule (enforce in the draw helper).\n- Audio: music at 10% is 0.10 of full-scale render; every SFX and song is non-silent; speakers differ.\n- Content lint: no `TODO` or placeholder text in `script.toml`, and every event has a card, rules and a bot.\n\n**`browser` (Playwright, one browser, 320x180, `file://`):** boots with no errors; `__gameStatus` appears; the pad stub moves focus and confirms; keyboard does the same; mouse hover moves focus and click activates, using rects the UI publishes in `window.__gameUi`; prompts switch with the device; Settings has the Xbox option; attract starts after 10 s and any input exits it; music reaches about 10% by the sound patch. Touch: Chromium with a phone and a tablet profile (it has WebGPU) must show \"Controller or keyboard required\", run attract, and respond to a Settings tap by `page.touchscreen.tap`. WebKit with an iPhone profile, which has no WebGPU here, must show the fallback message from section 11.\n\n**`hub`:** copy the page and `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` into one served folder, open `harness.html?game=<page>&players=N&auto=1`, wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === \"true\"`, and require `window.__hubCheck.pass`. Run 2 players; 3 and 4 players with `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`; and `scenario=host-leave`. Then a solo check like the hub's: open outside a session, press Enter and Space, expect no errors, `AIHub.active` false, and `Math.random` not seeded. Also byte-compare the pasted client with `hub/session.js`.\n\n**Second player:** the harness's copies, the only multi-player check possible on one computer. **TV:** judge 1920x1080 `pav capture`s.\n\n**Screenshots** only for what scripts cannot check: `pav capture` for composition, `filmstrip` and `animsheet` for motion, one 1080p sheet per set at the end. Do not use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n## 17. Build order\n\nEach step ends with its tests passing. Early steps close the risks first.\n\n1. **Setup.** Pick the title. `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall engine` (it is public). Copy the starter to `games/fit/`, register it, build `pav` and the web page, first `tests/run.sh`.\n2. **Seams.** Hide the chrome, fit input layout, `lean`, fonts, `fit_ui` with a Title and a Pause, Flow state machine, `weblink`, `__gameStatus`, save, a one-event Medley stub, hub JS. Get the harness and the browser smoke green now, before content exists.\n3. **First slice.** Balance Core, Puck, tilt, `studio` set, Pose Parade, event card and result, bot, one result screen in full art. Play it by bot and capture it. Tune until a ring streak feels good.\n4. **Island and voice.** `hub` set and props, walking, interactables, dialogue with syllables, audio buses, Lobby Bossa, Pip, the Morning Check, Balance Age, Calendar, Clam.\n5. **Events** in this order: Cannonball Cup, Paddle Slalom, Tide Table, Tempo Squats, Breath Bloom, Step Party (with sequencer and rhythm clock), Morning Lap, Plank Standoff, Floatie Spin, Tray Trek.\n6. **Meta.** Days, unlocks, titles, wardrobe, flip-flops, Yesterday You, absence comments, all songs and SFX.\n7. **Finale and endings.** Big Stand, both endings, credits, Free Play.\n8. **Shell.** Attract, Settings, Dev Mode, touch-only card, How to Play, About.\n9. **Multiplayer.** Full Medley, hub ghosts in every lane, Play Together.\n10. **Polish.** Write the script to full length, feel pass on every event using the sliders, extra set dressing, particles, transitions, stingers.\n11. **Finish** (section 18).\n\n**If time runs short,** cut in this order and remove all traces, leaving no stubs: Tray Trek, Floatie Spin, Plank Standoff. The roster, day table, attract list and Schedule all read from data, so a removed event leaves no hole; move its day's minute goal down to match. Everything else is required. Nothing may ship half-working; your brief says no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n\n## 18. Finishing\n\n1. Write the exit interview your brief names. Fill every field honestly; `usage` is exactly \"added on import\". Suggested `aiHub` values: type `Game`, genre `Sports`, source `Wii Fit`, series `Wii Fit`, players `1–4`, tags from `3d`, `third-person`, `comedy`, `sports`, `versus`, `low-poly`. Check that it parses.\n2. Run `tools/build.sh`: copy the exit interview into `about-data` in the page, run the single-file build with your title, description and theme colour, and write the HTML where your brief says. The About screen shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback from that block, including the fonts' and motion libraries' credits (`engine/store/CREDITS.md`, each clip set's `credit`).\n3. Run `tests/run.sh all` on the final page. Open it once from `file://` at 1920x1080 and look at one frame.\n4. Put the same Source Information and feedback in your chat reply.\n5. Delete `engine/target` and other caches. Leave the source, tests and notes in the dev folder.\n\n### What each brief item maps to\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
