{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_478","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,145p' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs'; rg -n 'device lost|DeviceLost|device_lost|destroy\\\\(|request_device|let .*device|\\\\.device\\\\s*=' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src' | head -100; sed -n '1,180p' 'Wii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/tests/run.sh'; rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|''^'\"\\\\| .*\\\\|' PLAN.md | head -120; tail -110 TASK.md\"","aggregated_output":"//! Window-bound graphics: surface, device, scene renderer and egui renderer.\n\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Context, Result, anyhow};\nuse pav_render::gpu::{self, BackendChoice};\nuse pav_render::wgpu;\nuse pav_render::{Renderer, Scene};\nuse winit::window::Window;\n\nuse crate::boot::{stage, stage_async};\n\npub struct Gfx {\n    _instance: wgpu::Instance,\n    pub window: Arc<Window>,\n    pub surface: wgpu::Surface<'static>,\n    pub adapter: wgpu::Adapter,\n    pub device: wgpu::Device,\n    pub queue: wgpu::Queue,\n    pub config: wgpu::SurfaceConfiguration,\n    pub renderer: Renderer,\n    pub egui: egui_wgpu::Renderer,\n    pub vsync: bool,\n    pub adapter_name: String,\n    pub gpu_errors: Arc<std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32>,\n}\n\nimpl Gfx {\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n    pub fn new(window: Arc<Window>, backend: BackendChoice, vsync: bool) -> Result<Self> {\n        pollster_block(Self::create(window, backend, vsync))\n    }\n\n    /// Creates everything; only the adapter and device requests wait (in the browser).\n    pub async fn create(window: Arc<Window>, backend: BackendChoice, vsync: bool) -> Result<Self> {\n        let web = cfg!(target_arch = \"wasm32\");\n        let instance = stage(\"graphics instance\", || {\n            Ok((gpu::create_instance(backend), format!(\"backend: {}\", if web { \"webgpu\" } else { backend.name() })))\n        })?;\n        let surface = stage(\"window surface\", || {\n            let s = instance.create_surface(window.clone()).context(\"could not create a drawing surface for the window\")?;\n            Ok((s, String::new()))\n        })?;\n        let adapter = stage_async(\"graphics adapter\", async {\n            if !web {\n                let all = gpu::list_adapters(&instance, backend);\n                for a in &all {\n                    log::info!(\"  found adapter: {}\", gpu::describe(a));\n                }\n                if all.is_empty() {\n                    return Err(anyhow!(\n                        \"no {} adapter found. Update your graphics driver{}\",\n                        backend.name(),\n                        if backend == BackendChoice::Vulkan { \", or try backend = \\\"dx12\\\" in shardfall.toml\" } else { \"\" }\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n            let a = gpu::request_adapter_async(&instance, Some(&surface)).await.map_err(|e| {\n                if web { anyhow!(\"{e:#}. This page needs a browser with WebGPU (a recent Chrome or Edge)\") } else { e }\n            })?;\n            let d = gpu::describe(&a.get_info());\n            Ok((a, d))\n        })\n        .await?;\n        let (device, queue) = stage_async(\"graphics device\", async {\n            let (d, q) = gpu::request_device_async(&adapter).await?;\n            Ok(((d, q), String::new()))\n        })\n        .await?;\n        let gpu_errors = Arc::new(std::sync::atomic::AtomicU32::new(0));\n        {\n            let errs = gpu_errors.clone();\n            device.on_uncaptured_error(Arc::new(move |e| {\n                errs.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed);\n                log::error!(\"GPU error: {e}\");\n            }));\n            device.set_device_lost_callback(|reason, msg| log::error!(\"GPU device lost ({reason:?}): {msg}\"));\n        }\n        let size = window.inner_size();\n        let config = stage(\"surface configure\", || {\n            let caps = surface.get_capabilities(&adapter);\n            let mut c = surface\n                .get_default_config(&adapter, size.width.max(1), size.height.max(1))\n                .context(\"surface not supported by adapter\")?;\n            // Non-sRGB target: the post pass encodes sRGB itself, and egui expects a linear-blend target.\n            c.format = caps.formats.iter().copied().find(|f| !f.is_srgb()).unwrap_or(c.format);\n            c.present_mode = present_mode(&caps, vsync);\n            surface.configure(&device, &c);\n            Ok((c.clone(), format!(\"{:?}, {:?}, {}x{}\", c.format, c.present_mode, c.width, c.height)))\n        })?;\n        let scope = device.push_error_scope(wgpu::ErrorFilter::Validation);\n        let renderer = stage(\"renderer & shaders\", || Ok((Renderer::new(&device, &queue), String::new())))?;\n        if let Some(e) = scope.pop().await {\n            return Err(anyhow!(\"shader/pipeline creation failed: {e}\"));\n        }\n        let egui = stage(\"ui renderer\", || {\n            Ok((egui_wgpu::Renderer::new(&device, config.format, egui_wgpu::RendererOptions::default()), String::new()))\n        })?;\n        let adapter_name = gpu::describe(&adapter.get_info());\n        Ok(Self { _instance: instance, window, surface, adapter, device, queue, config, renderer, egui, vsync, adapter_name, gpu_errors })\n    }\n\n    pub fn resize(&mut self, w: u32, h: u32) {\n        if w == 0 || h == 0 {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.config.width = w;\n        self.config.height = h;\n        self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n    }\n\n    pub fn set_vsync(&mut self, on: bool) {\n        self.vsync = on;\n        let caps = self.surface.get_capabilities(&self.adapter);\n        self.config.present_mode = present_mode(&caps, on);\n        self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n    }\n\n    /// Acquires the next surface texture, reconfiguring when needed. None = skip this frame.\n    pub fn acquire(&mut self) -> Option<wgpu::SurfaceTexture> {\n        match self.surface.get_current_texture() {\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Success(t) => Some(t),\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Suboptimal(t) => {\n                // Use this frame, reconfigure for the next one.\n                self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n                Some(t)\n            }\n            wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Outdated | wgpu::CurrentSurfaceTexture::Lost => {\n                self.surface.configure(&self.device, &self.config);\n                None\n            }\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn size(&self) -> (u32, u32) {\n        (self.config.width, self.config.height)\n    }\n\n    /// Renders the scene plus the egui overlay and presents. Texture updates are always\n    /// applied (and the delta cleared), even when the frame is skipped.\n    pub fn draw(&mut self, scene: &Scene, ui: Option<(&[egui::ClippedPrimitive], &mut egui::TexturesDelta, f32)>) -> bool {\n        let mut ui = ui;\n        if let Some((_, textures, _)) = ui.as_mut() {\n            for (id, deltas) in &textures.set {\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/rooms.rs:124:                let primary = match def.primary_device {\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:65:        let (device, queue) = stage_async(\"graphics device\", async {\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:66:            let (d, q) = gpu::request_device_async(&adapter).await?;\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:77:            device.set_device_lost_callback(|reason, msg| log::error!(\"GPU device lost ({reason:?}): {msg}\"));\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:91:        let scope = device.push_error_scope(wgpu::ErrorFilter::Validation);\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:92:        let renderer = stage(\"renderer & shaders\", || Ok((Renderer::new(&device, &queue), String::new())))?;\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/gfx.rs:155:            let mut encoder = self.device.create_command_encoder(&wgpu::CommandEncoderDescriptor { label: Some(\"frame\") });\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:399:            let st = UiInput::new(&self.egui_ctx, &window, gfx.device.limits().max_texture_dimension_2d as usize);\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:404:                let d = format!(\"{} @ {} Hz\", a.device, a.sample_rate);\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:983:        let pad = self.input.last_device == Device::Gamepad;\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:1183:        let aim = match self.input.last_device {\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:1340:        let device = self.input.last_device;\nWii Fit Together-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-222701/engine/crates/pav_app/src/app.rs:1341:        let touch_ui = device == Device::Touch;\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nexport PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64\nexec python3 \"$(dirname \"$0\")/test_game.py\" \"${1:-all}\"\n5:## 0. How to read this plan\n15:## 1. The game in one page\n23:| What makes Wii Fit distinctive | How this game turns it into play |\n25:| A balance board: your centre of pressure is the only verb, and tiny movements matter | The Balance Core (section 4): one analog dot driven by lean. Every event is played through it |\n26:| The body test and the verdict of a fitness age | The Morning Wobble Check and Balance Age. Never weight, BMI or body shape |\n27:| Equipment that talks: cheerful, polite, quietly rude | Pip, a talking wobble cushion, plus a whole staff of talking gear |\n28:| A calm, uncanny instructor | Posey, a faceless wooden artist's mannequin |\n29:| Four disciplines: yoga, strength, aerobics, balance games | Four pavilions, each with its own coach and events |\n30:| The daily ritual: calendar stamps, banked minutes that unlock more | Calendar stamps and the Minute Clam |\n31:| Noticing how long you stayed away | Real-date absence comments from Pip |\n32:| A family sharing one board and comparing progress | Hot-seat Medley, Yesterday You ghosts, AI Hub sessions |\n33:| Clean, quiet, pastel, unintentionally funny | High-key pastel look, gentle lounge music, short deadpan text |\n48:## 2. Structure of play\n60:| Day | Opens | Minute goal | Story beat |\n62:| 1 | Stillness Studio: Breath Bloom, Pose Parade | 3 | Arrival. The checkout rule. \"Nobody has asked to leave yet\" |\n63:| 2 | Iron Gazebo: Tempo Squats, Plank Standoff | 5 | Big Kettle. Wardrobe opens |\n64:| 3 | Boardwalk: Step Party, Floatie Spin, Morning Lap | 7 | Yesterday You appears as a ghost. \"I am not comparing you\" |\n65:| 4 | Lagoon: Tide Table, Paddle Slalom, Tray Trek, Cannonball Cup | 9 | Guests start whispering about the Big Stand |\n66:| 5 | The Big Stand, after the morning check | none | Finale, ending, free play |\n74:## 3. Controls\n78:| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n80:| Walk on the island, lean in events | Left stick | W A S D (arrows also walk) | Island: click a place or thing to walk there. Events: cursor offset from screen centre is the lean |\n81:| Fine lean | Stick itself | Hold Shift (half key rate) | Small cursor moves |\n82:| Jog on the island | Hold R2 | Hold Shift | |\n83:| Action: talk, begin, inhale, squat, bounce | Cross | Space | Left button |\n84:| Left step / right step | L1 or L2 / R1 or R2 | Q or Left / E or Right | Left button / right button |\n85:| Brace (hold both) | L2+R2 or L1+R1 | Q+E | Both buttons |\n86:| Wave at someone | Square | F | |\n87:| Schedule | Triangle | Tab | |\n88:| Island camera | Right stick | | Right-drag, wheel zooms |\n89:| Pause | Options | Esc | |\n90:| Menus | D-pad or left stick, Cross confirms, Circle backs, L1/R1 change tabs | Arrows or W A S D, Enter or Space, Esc backs, Q/E change tabs | Hover moves focus, click activates, right-click backs |\n94:## 4. The Balance Core\n119:## 5. Events\n125:### Stillness Studio (Posey). Set: `studio`, a round white deck on a calm dawn lagoon. Camera: mirror view (yaw 0, tilt 12, distance 8.5, fov 36; the figure faces +Z, toward the camera, and stick right is screen right).\n131:### Iron Gazebo (Big Kettle). Set: `gazebo`, a white iron gazebo at golden hour. Camera: 3/4 front.\n137:### Boardwalk (Bounce). Set: `boardwalk`, a beach boardwalk at bright midday.\n145:### Lagoon (Commodore Gull). Sets: `tide`, `channel`, `pier`, `tower`.\n155:### The Big Stand (finale, about 2.5 minutes). Set: `bay`, a tiny post in the bay at sunset, with the whole cast watching from rafts.\n166:### Morning Wobble Check. Set: `studio`. About 70 s, four micro-tests, Pip presiding.\n168:| Test | What you do | Metric (0 to 1) |\n170:| Stand Still (\"This is the whole test\") | Hold the dot centred for 8 s; one small nudge at 5 s | Mean distance from centre |\n171:| Lean Targets | A left/right bar: reach \"70% left\", then two more targets, within 6%, hold 1.5 s; 5 s limit each | Time taken |\n172:| Eyes Closed | 6 s. The screen dims to almost black, the Puck vanishes. A tone's pitch tells your offset, and rumble if the pad has it | Mean distance |\n173:| Step Test | 8 alternating steps to a 100 BPM metronome | Timing |\n181:## 6. Meta systems\n192:## 7. World, cast and writing\n196:| Character | What it is | Voice and manner |\n198:| **Pip** | Sky-blue round wobble cushion, two dot eyes (`blob` plan, scale 0.7, squash 2.0, plus a small valve) | Front desk and scorekeeper. Relentlessly pleasant. Keeps records of everything. Never technically insults you. Squeaky rubber voice |\n199:| **Posey** | Faceless wooden artist's mannequin (biped with eyes the colour of its skin; verified look in section 8) | Stillness. Serene, short, oddly poetic. Soft wood-block voice. It has no eyes and never blinks; \"This has not been discussed\" |\n200:| **Big Kettle** | Cast-iron kettlebell (dark `blob` plus a torus handle part; add a `Handle` attach kind) | Strength. Booming, gentle, deeply supportive. No arms or legs, cannot do any exercise |\n201:| **Bounce** | Huge pink exercise ball that never stops hopping (`blob`, scale 1.2, `hop`) | Cardio. Breathless bursts, no full stops |\n202:| **Commodore Gull** | Small gull in a captain's cap: three no-physics entities, wings rotated in code | Lagoon. Dry, officious, steals snacks |\n203:| **Marjorie** | Guest in robe and towel turban (`helm = hood`, white) | On Day 5 for eleven years. Knows everything |\n204:| **Two-Towel Tom** | Guest with two towels | Lost his flip-flops. Asks you to find them |\n205:| **The Twins** | Two identical guests | Do everything in unison, finish each other's words |\n227:## 8. Art direction\n283:## 9. Animation\n308:## 10. Audio\n319:| Song | Where | Feel (all original) |\n321:| 1 Lobby Bossa | Title, island | 84 BPM, soft nylon-like pluck, brushed noise, walking bass: resort lounge, not gym |\n322:| 2 Tide Pad | Stillness | Slow pads and sparse bells, no beat |\n323:| 3 Iron Waltz | Strength | 3/4, low pizzicato bass, a dignified tuba-like square |\n324:| 4 Step Party | Step Party, Floatie Spin | 96 BPM funk-pop, clear kick and clap for timing |\n325:| 5 Lagoon Mallets | Lagoon | 110 BPM marimba-like triangle, playful |\n326:| 6 Band of Guests | Big Stand | Marching brass and snare, then fades to silence |\n327:| 7 Stingers | Results, stamps, stars, reveal | Short jingles |\n333:## 11. UI and UX\n343:| Screen | Content |\n345:| Title | Logo in Fredoka over the island sweep, 4 options |\n346:| Guest select, wardrobe | Big cards, live figure on a turntable |\n347:| Dialogue | Bottom card, speaker name, 2 lines, advance glyph |\n348:| Island HUD | Day and minute goal top left, Balance Age top right, a world-anchored prompt on the nearest interactable |\n349:| Schedule | Carousel of pavilions, then events, with stars. Fast travel |\n350:| Event HUD | Puck, score, timer, one prompt row |\n351:| Result | Score, stars, title, minutes, best, Retry or Back |\n352:| Check report | Giant rolling number, sparkline, Pip's comment |\n353:| Calendar | Five big days and stamps |\n354:| Pause | Resume, Schedule, Calendar, Wardrobe, Settings, How to Play, About, Dev Mode, Quit to Title |\n355:| Settings | Music (default 10%), SFX, Voices, Glyphs, Lean Sensitivity, Wobble Assist, Rhythm Offset, Text Speed, Fullscreen, Reset Save (with confirm) |\n356:| About | Pages for each block your brief requires (section 18) |\n357:| Play Together | Choose 2 to 4 guests, pass-the-pad interstitial, podium |\n371:## 12. Attract mode\n377:## 13. Multiplayer\n443:## 14. Architecture\n489:| File | Change | Why |\n491:| `pav_core/src/input.rs` | Add to `InputFrame`: `lean: Vec2`, `any: bool`, `ui: Option<UiEvent>` (Copy: `Hover(u16)`, `Click(u16)`, `Slider(u16, f32)`), `song_pos: Option<f32>`, all `#[serde(default)]`. New `buttons`: CONFIRM, BACK, MENU_UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT, TAB_L, TAB_R, PAUSE, SCHEDULE, WAVE, STEP_L, STEP_R, HUB_GO | Everything the game needs is input, so replays and tests cover it |\n492:| `pav_core/src/games/mod.rs`, `sim.rs` | `Game::frozen()` with a default of false (add it to the trait and its `DynGame` and `GameBox` wrappers); when true, `step_inner` skips everything after `play_pre` except tick++ and events | Pause freezes the world while menus still run |\n493:| `pav_core/src/frame.rs`, `sim.rs` | `HudFrame.status: Option<String>` (the game's status JSON), `HudFrame.fit: Option<Arc<UiFrame>>` and `HudFrame.camera_yaw: Option<f32>`, which `pav_app` and `pav_tools/src/session.rs` apply to the rig each frame | Link to JS and to the UI; Morning Lap's chase camera, since cues blend over 0.7 s and cannot follow per tick |\n494:| `pav_view/src/build.rs`, `fx.rs` | Handle `BigSplash` (bigger water dents and bursts) and a silent `Wake { pos }` event (a small dent, no sound; `Splash` is loud if repeated) | The dive's payoff and the paddle's wake |\n495:| `pav_core/src/puppet.rs` | `tilt_side`, `tilt_fwd` in `PuppetState` (add to `lerp`), applied in `body_motion` as a rotation about the feet | The whole figure leans with the dot |\n496:| `pav_core/src/parts.rs` | `AttachKind::Handle` | Kettle |\n497:| `pav_ui/src/lib.rs`, `headless.rs`, `fit_ui.rs` | Draw `fit_ui` from both window and headless; `has_ui` true when `hud.fit` is set; install fonts before the first pass | Captures show the real UI |\n498:| `pav_tools/src/tools.rs` (`t_input`) | Accept `lean=[x,y]` | Poke events from the REPL |\n499:| `pav_app/src/input.rs` | `Bindings::fit()`, a fit layout in `poll_gamepad` (Cross, Circle, L1/L2, R1/R2, D-pad taps, Options unless Create is held), key ramps for lean, menu repeat | Controller-first layout |\n500:| `pav_app/src/app.rs` | When `hud.fit` is set: hide `boot_panel`, `stats_panel`, `guide_panel`, the room card, `hint_bar` and the F1 panel (verified necessary); route Esc and Options to PAUSE instead of `set_menu`; gate `handle_key`; call `weblink` each frame; apply Settings to audio buses; save to `localStorage` when the save serial changes; keep fit bindings in the room-change branch, which otherwise rebinds keys | Removes the engine demo's chrome and keys |\n501:| `pav_audio` | Section 10 | Music, voices, buses |\n502:| `web/index.html` | Client, tags, JS, `about-data`, catalogue `<title>Name — Tagline</title>`, description, theme colour, an inline SVG icon; remove the phone hints | Hub and catalogue |\n520:## 15. Verified facts and traps\n557:## 16. Testing\n582:## 17. Build order\n600:## 18. Finishing\n608:### What each brief item maps to\n610:| Brief item | Section |\n612:| Genre and perspective | 1 |\n613:| Title method | 0, 17 |\n614:| Research | Optional. The plan already reflects a short web search on Wii Fit, and that is enough; report what you actually did |\n615:| Single HTML package | 14, 15, 18 (no external dependencies are needed) |\n616:| Build on the engine, change anything | 14 |\n617:| Art direction, new assets, library for generics | 8 |\n618:| Animation | 9 |\n619:| Audio in code, gibberish voices, music at 10% | 10, 16 |\n620:| Online and multiplayer, in the hub and on its own | 13, 16 |\n621:| Dev folder, bundling, shared machine, ports and processes | 0, 15, 16; your brief has the rules |\n622:| Performance out of scope, as much detail as the scene needs | 8, 15 |\n623:| One test command, scripted playthrough, screenshots only when needed, no review agents | 16 |\n624:| Stand-ins: pad, touch, sound, second player, iPhone, TV | 15, 16 |\n625:| Original game, feature-complete vertical slice, extendable with normal tools | 1, 14 (all data in TOML, JSON clips and props; `README.md` explains adding an event, a set and a song) |\n626:| Dev mode from pause, live sliders, reset | 11 |\n627:| Attract mode | 12 |\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:27:01\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-terra, reasoning effort max\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
